# Milestone 3: AI-Powered Requirement Understanding and Unified Requirement Specification (URS)

## Project Context

I am building an Autonomous Software Testing Agent called Autonomous QA Studio.

The existing application has:
- A Angular frontend.
- A Python FastAPI backend.
- OpenAI integration.
- Plain-text test case generation.
- Multi-file upload and document extraction capabilities from the previous milestone.

The previous milestone introduced extraction of:
- PDF text and embedded images.
- DOCX paragraphs, headings, tables and embedded images.
- XLSX sheets, rows and embedded images.
- CSV data.
- TXT content.
- PPTX slides, text and embedded images.
- Standalone images and UI mockups.

Now I want to build an AI-powered requirement understanding engine that analyzes all this extracted information and converts it into a structured Unified Requirement Specification (URS).

## 1. Inspect the Existing Implementation

Before writing code:

1. Inspect the complete repository.
2. Understand the existing document ingestion and extraction services.
3. Inspect how extracted text and images are stored.
4. Inspect the existing OpenAI integration.
5. Understand existing database models and API conventions.
6. Inspect the frontend upload and generated-content pages.
7. Identify incomplete features from the previous milestone.

Important:
- Reuse the existing extraction services.
- Do not implement document extraction again.
- Do not replace existing APIs.
- Do not break plain-text test generation.
- Do not introduce a duplicate database or unnecessary infrastructure.
- Extend the existing architecture.

## 2. Build the AI Requirement Understanding Engine

Create a modular service responsible for understanding extracted requirement content.

It must accept:
- Extracted paragraphs and headings.
- Tables and spreadsheet data.
- User stories.
- Acceptance criteria.
- Business rules.
- Actual extracted images.
- UI screenshots and mockups.
- Diagrams and flowcharts.
- Source metadata.

The engine must understand the relationship between textual and visual information.

Use the existing OpenAI SDK and configured model.

Use a vision-capable model for image analysis. Do not assume that the currently configured text model supports image inputs.

Keep model configuration environment-driven.

Do not hardcode API keys or model-specific assumptions throughout the codebase.

## 3. Requirement Extraction

Extract atomic, meaningful requirements from the available content.

Each requirement should contain:

- Unique requirement ID.
- Requirement title.
- Detailed description.
- Requirement category.
- Requirement type.
- Priority.
- Actor or user role, if identifiable.
- Preconditions, if identifiable.
- Business rules.
- Acceptance criteria.
- Related UI components.
- Related screens.
- Source references.
- Confidence level.
- Assumptions.
- Unresolved questions.

Supported requirement categories:

- Functional.
- Non-functional.
- Validation.
- Security.
- Performance.
- Usability.
- UI/UX.
- Business rules.
- Integration.
- Accessibility.

Do not invent information that is not present in the source material.

If a field cannot be determined, return null or an appropriate unknown status.

## 4. User Story Understanding

The engine must recognize user story formats such as:

"As a [user role], I want [functionality], so that [business benefit]."

Extract:

- Actor.
- User goal.
- Business benefit.
- Acceptance criteria.
- Functional requirements.
- Validation rules.
- Relevant edge cases explicitly supported by the story.

Support user stories with and without acceptance criteria.

If acceptance criteria are missing:
- Extract what can be established.
- Identify missing information.
- Create clarification questions.
- Do not silently invent business behavior.

## 5. UI Mockup and Screenshot Understanding

This is a critical feature.

The system must analyze actual images extracted in the previous milestone.

Use a vision-capable LLM to identify:

- Input fields.
- Buttons.
- Links.
- Dropdowns.
- Checkboxes.
- Radio buttons.
- Navigation elements.
- Labels and placeholders.
- Required indicators.
- Error messages visible in the design.
- Component relationships.
- Screen structure.
- Visual hierarchy.
- Relevant layout information.

For each identified UI component, capture:

- Component ID.
- Component type.
- Visible label.
- Placeholder, if present.
- Visible state.
- Associated screen.
- Source image ID.
- Relevant source location.

Example:

{
  "screen_id": "SCREEN_001",
  "screen_name": "User Registration",
  "source_image_id": "IMG_001",
  "components": [
    {
      "component_id": "UI_001",
      "type": "input",
      "label": "Email",
      "input_type": "email"
    },
    {
      "component_id": "UI_002",
      "type": "input",
      "label": "Password",
      "input_type": "password"
    },
    {
      "component_id": "UI_003",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Register"
    }
  ]
}

Important rules:

- Do not infer backend behavior from visual appearance alone.
- Do not assume validation rules that are not specified.
- Do not assume that a visible button performs a particular action unless supported by context.
- Mark inferred observations separately from confirmed requirements.
- Preserve the original image reference.
- Allow one screen to have multiple related images.

## 6. Multimodal Context Processing

The system must combine related text and images.

Example:

A DOCX document contains:

Heading: User Registration

Text:
"Users should register using their email address and password."

Embedded screenshot:
Registration form containing email, password and register button.

The system should analyze the text and screenshot together.

However, preserve the distinction between:

1. Explicit requirements from text.
2. Visual observations from images.
3. Inferences from combined context.
4. Unresolved questions.

Do not send only image descriptions to the vision model. Where visual understanding is required, provide the actual image.

For documents with multiple screenshots, maintain image IDs and source locations.

## 7. Large Document Processing

Some documents may contain hundreds of pages.

Implement a scalable processing approach:

- Use existing extracted content.
- Organize content by document, page, section, sheet or slide.
- Use token-aware and section-aware chunking.
- Preserve source metadata across chunks.
- Process related sections together where possible.
- Process images alongside their relevant textual context.
- Avoid sending an entire large document in one request.
- Avoid losing relationships between sections and images.

Implement controlled concurrency and handle model rate limits.

Support retries for temporary API failures.

Do not repeatedly process already completed content unnecessarily.

If processing fails for one chunk, preserve successful results and report the failure.

## 8. Requirement Consolidation Engine

After extracting requirements from individual sources, consolidate them into one project-level specification.

Implement:

### A. Duplicate Detection

Identify requirements that express the same business behavior.

Do not delete source references when combining duplicates.

### B. Conflict Detection

Identify contradictory requirements.

Example:

Document A:
"Password must contain at least 8 characters."

Document B:
"Password must contain at least 12 characters."

The system must:
- Identify the conflict.
- Preserve both original statements.
- Show their source documents and locations.
- Mark the requirement as requiring human resolution.

Never silently select one version.

### C. Missing Information Detection

Identify:
- Missing acceptance criteria.
- Undefined validation rules.
- Unclear workflows.
- Missing error handling behavior.
- Incomplete user journeys.
- Unclear UI-to-business-rule relationships.

### D. Requirement Relationships

Identify related requirements and potential dependencies.

Preserve original requirement IDs and source traceability.

## 9. Unified Requirement Specification (URS)

Create a consistent Pydantic schema for the complete specification.

Example:

{
  "project_id": "PROJECT_001",
  "specification_id": "URS_001",
  "status": "pending_review",
  "requirements": [
    {
      "requirement_id": "REQ_001",
      "title": "User Registration",
      "description": "Users must register using their email address.",
      "category": "functional",
      "priority": "high",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "evidence_type": "explicit",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        "Users must enter a valid email address."
      ],
      "related_ui_components": [
        "UI_001"
      ],
      "source_references": [
        {
          "file_id": "FILE_001",
          "filename": "Registration_SRS.docx",
          "section": "User Registration"
        }
      ],
      "assumptions": [],
      "unresolved_questions": []
    }
  ],
  "conflicts": [],
  "duplicates": [],
  "clarification_questions": []
}

This is an illustrative schema. Adapt it to the actual application.

Requirements must have stable IDs and source traceability.

## 10. Persistence

Persist the generated specification using the existing database architecture.

Store:
- Project ID.
- Specification ID and version.
- Extracted requirements.
- Source references.
- UI component specifications.
- Conflicts.
- Duplicate relationships.
- Clarification questions.
- Approval status.
- Creation and update timestamps.

Support retrieving previously generated specifications.

Avoid storing large image binary data inside ordinary requirement records. Use existing file storage and image references.

## 11. Backend APIs

Create APIs following the existing FastAPI conventions.

Suggested endpoints:

POST /api/requirements/analyze

Input:
- Project ID.
- Uploaded file IDs or references to previously extracted documents.
- Optional additional plain-text requirements.

Behavior:
- Retrieve previously extracted content.
- Analyze relevant text and images.
- Generate structured requirements.
- Consolidate requirements.
- Save the resulting URS.
- Return its specification ID and processing status.

GET /api/requirements/specifications/{specification_id}

Retrieve a complete URS.

GET /api/requirements/specifications/{specification_id}/review

Retrieve requirements, conflicts, duplicates and clarification questions for review.

PATCH /api/requirements/specifications/{specification_id}/requirements/{requirement_id}

Allow editing a requirement during human review.

POST /api/requirements/specifications/{specification_id}/approve

Approve the specification only when required conflicts have been resolved.

Adapt endpoint names and schemas to the existing project structure.

## 12. Frontend Integration

Extend the existing Autonomous QA Studio frontend.

Add a Requirement Analysis and Review interface.

Display:

### A. Analysis Summary
- Total extracted requirements.
- Functional requirements.
- Validation requirements.
- Security requirements.
- UI/UX requirements.
- Number of conflicts.
- Number of duplicate candidates.
- Number of clarification questions.

### B. Requirement Explorer

Show requirements in a searchable and filterable table.

Columns:
- Requirement ID.
- Title.
- Category.
- Priority.
- Confidence.
- Evidence type.
- Review status.
- Source.

### C. Requirement Details

When a user opens a requirement, show:
- Description.
- Acceptance criteria.
- Business rules.
- Related UI components.
- Source document.
- Page, section or sheet.
- Related image references.
- Assumptions.
- Unresolved questions.

### D. Conflict Review

Display conflicting requirements side by side.

Show:
- Original statements.
- Source documents.
- Source locations.
- Reason for conflict.

Allow the user to resolve the conflict by editing or selecting the authoritative requirement.

Do not automatically resolve conflicts.

### E. Clarification Questions

Display questions generated by the AI.

Allow the user to provide answers and save them as additional confirmed requirements or acceptance criteria.

### F. Approval

Provide:
- Approve specification.
- Save changes.
- Return for clarification.

A specification must not be approved while unresolved blocking conflicts remain.

Use the existing UI design and reusable components.

## 13. AI Reliability and Validation

The LLM output must be validated against Pydantic schemas.

Implement:
- Structured output where supported by the configured model.
- JSON parsing and validation.
- Controlled retries for malformed output.
- Safe error handling.
- Logging without exposing API keys or sensitive document contents.
- Confidence values with a consistent interpretation.

Do not allow the model to fabricate source references.

Every extracted requirement must reference actual source content or be explicitly identified as an inference.

Confidence scores must not be treated as proof of correctness.

